  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1148 AM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nToday through Monday...\n\nHigh pressure settled into the region today, allowing for sunny \nskies and warm temperatures rising from the 30s and 40s close \nto 60 by noon today. High temperatures are expected to continue \nrising into the mid and upper 60s by late afternoon. Lows \ntonight will dip into the 40s, with some light patchy fog \npossible in valleys and low lying areas, especially near the KS \nand MO borders. \n\nA low pressure system sliding across south-central Canada will drag \na dry cold front through the region late Monday. Southerly flow \nahead of the approaching front will drive temperatures well into the \nmid and upper 60s, with a few locations in southeast Nebraska \nreaching the low 70s. Temperatures fall into the mid 30s and low 40s \nbehind the front Monday night.\n\nTuesday through Thursday...\n\nTuesday into Wednesday, a shortwave trough will move from the \nPacific NW through the central CONUS. Meanwhile, a separate low will \nchurn through the Ark-La-Tex region, eating up golf moisture, before \nit can reach the central and northern Plains. While a small portion \nof guidance wants to bring showers through the forecast area with \nthe shortwave passage, the vast majority of ensemble members keep \nsufficient moisture off to our east and southeast.\n\nTemperatures will begin a downward trend Tuesday through the end of \nthe work week. Highs will drop from the low 60s Tuesday, to the \nupper 50s Wednesday, down into the upper 40s by Thursday. \n\nThursday Night and Beyond...\n\nThe Tuesday/Wednesday shortwave will continue to dive south late \nweek, as a notable trough digs through the eastern half of the \ncountry. This trough will also bring a surge of cooler air to the \neastern states, leaving our forecast area on its periphery. \nOvernight lows could fall below freezing Thursday night/early \nFriday. The latest ensemble runs have trended a touch cooler, \nalthough the entire forecast area remains near the cusp, just under \n32 degrees. However a slight eastward shift to the trough could push \nus a few degrees warmer, into the mid 30s, while a small shift to \nthe west would push us into the mid 20s. \n\nThe trough is expected to continue progressing eastward next \nweekend, with a warming trend and continued dry weather expected \nbehind it. If Omaha doesn't dip below freezing by Friday morning, we \nwill likely blow well past the current record for latest fall \nfreeze.",
